.TH "SERVAL" "1" "Aug 2026" "Serval" "Serval Manual"

.SH NAME
serval-inspect - Analyze the blast radius of a file or directory


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBserval inspect  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Analyze a file's direct and indirect dependents within the dependency
graph, plus Git history, relevant CI workflows, and an explainable risk
score. Given a directory (e.g. "serval inspect ." or "serval
inspect src"), every recognized module inside it is analyzed and
reported as a risk-sorted summary instead of one full per-file report.
 defaults to "." (the current directory) if omitted. See
"serval --help" for the v0.1 module-resolution scope and
limitations (JavaScript/TypeScript, Go, Python).


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--explain\fP[=false]
	ask an AI provider to explain each result's risk in natural language (one call per file — can be slow across a directory)

.PP
\fB--explain-host\fP=""
	Ollama host, --explain-provider=ollama only (default: $OLLAMA_HOST or http://localhost:11434)

.PP
\fB--explain-model\fP=""
	model to use (default: llama3.2 for ollama, the provider's own default otherwise)

.PP
\fB--explain-provider\fP="ollama"
	explain provider: ollama (local daemon), claude, codex, or gemini (local CLI, already authenticated)

.PP
\fB--fail-on\fP=""
	exit with code 2 if risk is at or above this level (low, medium, high)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for inspect

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	output machine-readable JSON

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP=""
	write the report to this file instead of stdout


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBserval(1)\fP


